SQL commands are commonly divided into 5 categories.
1. DDL (Data Definition Language)
Used to create and modify database objects.
Command
Purpose
CREATE
Create a database, table, view, index, etc.
ALTER
Modify an existing table or object
DROP
Delete a table, database, view, etc. permanently
TRUNCATE
Remove all rows from a table (keeps table structure)
RENAME
Rename a table or other object
COMMENT
Add comments to database objects (supported in some DBMS)
Example:
CREATE TABLE Student (
    id INT,
    name VARCHAR(50)
);

ALTER TABLE Student
ADD age INT;

DROP TABLE Student;
2. DML (Data Manipulation Language)
Used to manipulate data inside tables.
Command
Purpose
INSERT
Add new records
UPDATE
Modify existing records
DELETE
Delete records
MERGE
Insert or update records (supported in some DBMS)
Example:
INSERT INTO Student
VALUES (1, 'John', 20);

UPDATE Student
SET age = 21
WHERE id = 1;

DELETE FROM Student
WHERE id = 1;
3. DQL (Data Query Language)
Used to retrieve data.
Command
Purpose
SELECT
Retrieve data from tables
Common clauses used with SELECT:
SELECT
FROM
WHERE
ORDER BY
GROUP BY
HAVING
DISTINCT
LIMIT
OFFSET
TOP
Example:
SELECT name, age
FROM Student
WHERE age > 18
ORDER BY age DESC;
4. DCL (Data Control Language)
Used to control user permissions.
Command
Purpose
GRANT
Give permissions
REVOKE
Remove permissions
Example:
GRANT SELECT
ON Student
TO user1;

REVOKE SELECT
ON Student
FROM user1;
5. TCL (Transaction Control Language)
Used to manage transactions.
Command
Purpose
COMMIT
Save changes permanently
ROLLBACK
Undo changes
SAVEPOINT
Create a rollback point
SET TRANSACTION
Configure transaction properties
Example:
UPDATE Student
SET age = 22
WHERE id = 1;

SAVEPOINT sp1;

DELETE FROM Student
WHERE id = 2;

ROLLBACK TO sp1;

COMMIT;
